{"G20700":"<b> μελισσό-φῠτον</b>, τό, <br /> = {μελισσόφυλλον}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 677 \">Refs 2nd c.BC+</a>].","G20701":"<b> μελοποι-ός</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>maker of songs, lyric poet</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1250, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 326a, etc.\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; ὁ Θηβαῖος μ., of Pindar, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 1.3c \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; ἡ Λεσβία μ., of Sappho, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Im.” 18. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> as <i>adjective</i>, generally, <b>tuneful</b>, μέριμνα [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 550 (Lyric poetry). \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20702":"<b> μερῐδάρχ-ης</b>, ου, ὁ, <br /> <b>governor of a district</b> or <b>province,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" PTeb. 66.60 (2nd c.BC), LXX.1Mac.10.65, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.5.5. \">LXX+2nd c.BC+</a>]","G20703":"<b> μερῐδαρχ-ία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>his office</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.1Esd.1.5, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 15.7.3. \">LXX+1st c.AD+</a>]","G20704":"<b> μεριζομένως</b>, <br /> <i>adverb</i>, gloss on{μελεϊστί}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"Scholia 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.281. \">Refs 3rd c.AD+</a>]","G20705":"<b> μερινων</b>, <br /> <i>genitive</i> <i>plural</i>, uncertain meaning in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “BGU” 1519 (from Ptolemaic)\">Refs</a>].","G20706":"<b> μερῑτ-εία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>division</b> of property, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “PFay.” 97.16 (1st c.AD). \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> ={μεριδαρχία}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, 9th c.AD: Photius Legal icographus, etc. (ubi μεριτία). \">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>]","G20707":"<b> μεροποσπόρος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>begetting men</b>, ὥρη [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.577. \">Refs 4th c.AD+</a>]","G20708":"<b> μεσάζω</b>, <br /> ={μεσόω}, ὁ μεσάζων τόπος (variant{νησίζων}) [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 1.32; πότερον ἄρχοιτο τὸ πάθος ἢ μεσάζοι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιστολαί” 18; νυκτὸς μεσαζούσης LXX.Wis.18.14; μεσαζούσης ἡμέρας 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 7.5.2 \">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>]; of food, <b>to be half-cooked</b>, Bilabel Ὀψαρτ.[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" p.11. \">Refs</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <i>passive</i>, <b>to be inserted in the middle, intervene</b>, αἱ μεσαζόμεναι λέξεις [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Apollonius Dyscolus Grammaticus “de Syntaxi;” 270.5, compare “Conj.” 255.20 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; of terms in an arithmetical series, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 39. \">Refs</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__II.2</b></Level3> <b>occupy a central position</b>, τὴν γῆν ὁ μῦθος λέγει μεσάζεσθαι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1389.38. \">Refs</a>]","G20709":"<b> μεσημβρ-ίζω</b>, <br /> ={μεσημβριάζω} I, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.21, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 7.2.1. \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]","G20710":"<b> μεσο-νεφής</b>, ές, <br /> <b>with clouds</b> <b>in the midst,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" Cat.Codex Astr. 8(1).138. \">Refs</a>]","G20711":"<b> μεσο-πορέω</b>, <br /> <b>to be half-way</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 1036, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 25.2, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 18.34, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 2.88 \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]: <i>metaphorically</i>, of a meal, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.34(31).21; also μεσοπορούσης τῆς κατὰ τὴν ὀπώραν ἀκμῆς 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.109. \">LXX+1st c.AD+</a>]","G20712":"<b> μεσο-πόρος</b>, <br /> <i>Epic dialect</i> <b>μεσσ-</b>, ον, <b>going</b> or <b>passing in the middle</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.46 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; μ. δι᾽ αἰθέρος through <b>mid</b>-air, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1152. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20713":"<b> μεσορρᾰγής</b>, ές, <br /> <b>rent in twain</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 2.31. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20714":"<b> μεταγίγνομαι</b>, <br /> later <b>Μεταγειτν-γίνομαι</b> [ῑ], <b>take place later</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “BGU” 1038.22 (2nd c.AD)\">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; <b>to be transferred, carried away</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.2Mac.2.1. \">LXX</a>]","G20715":"<b> μεταδῐαιτάω</b>, <br /> <b>change one's way of life</b>, εἰς δίαιταν Μηδικὴν μ. ἑαυτόν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 12.3 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]:—<i>passive</i>, μεταδιαιτηθείς [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.4Mac.8.8. \">LXX</a>]","G20716":"<b> μετακιρνάω</b>, <br /> ={μετακεράννυμι}, in <i>middle</i>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Wis.16.21. \">LXX</a>]","G20717":"<b> μεταλλ-ευτός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>to be got by mining</b>, τὰ μ., opposed to τὰ ὀρυκτά, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 378a21, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.166. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]","G20718":"<b> μετανά-στᾰτος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>devastated by migrations</b>, ἡ Ἑλλὰς οὐχ ὑπ᾽ ἀνθρώπων μόνον γινομένη μ. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC(?): Ocellus Lucanus Philosophus 3.5. \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <b>having departed</b>, πατέρων μετανάστατος ψυχή [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.513. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20719":"<b> μεταπαιδ-εύω</b>, <br /> <b>educate differently</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.4Mac.2.7 (_passive_), 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Anach.” 17 \">LXX+2nd c.AD+</a>]:—<i>passive</i>, of a substance, <b>acquire a fresh tendency</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" Palladius Medicus “in Hippocratem” 2.104 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]","G20720":"<b> μετατεύχω</b>, <br /> <b>refashion</b>, αὐτὸς ἑαυτὸν εἰς μάχιμον μ. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 612.10. \">Refs</a>]","G20721":"<b> μετεξ-έτεροι</b>, αι, α, <br /> <i>Ionic dialect</i> <i>pronoun</i>, ={ἔνιοι}, <b>some among many, certain</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.63, 95, 199, +others, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 11, +others\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; χρῆσις μετεξετέρη <b>a certain amount of</b> use, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" [prev. author] “Art.” 52. (μετ᾽ ἐξετέρην\">Refs</a>] should be written divisim in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 588.) \">Refs 2nd c.BC+</a>]","G20722":"<b> μετεωρο-πορία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>travelling through air</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 636.38. \">Refs</a>]","G20723":"<b> μετοιᾱκίζομαι</b>, <br /> <i>passive</i>, <b>have one's course changed</b>, ὁ ὑφ᾽ ἡδονῆς δεῦρο κἀκεῖ μετοιακιζόμενος [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.34a. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20724":"<b> μετοίκ-ησις</b>, εως, ἡ, <br /> = {μετοικία} 1, μ. τοῦ τόπου τοῦ ἐνθένδε εἰς ἄλλον τόπον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 40c; τὴν μ. τὴν ἐνθένδε ἐκεῖσε [prev. author] “Phd.” 117c, compare “Cat.Codex Astr.” 7.110. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__2</b></Level3> <i>metaphorically</i>, of the <b>transmigration</b> of souls, τὴν ἀρίστην μ. τὴν τοῦ ἀνθρώπου.. λέοντα γίνεσθαι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.7. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> =[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" {μετοικία} 11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 850a. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20725":"<b> μετοχετ-εύω</b>, <br /> <b>convey in a channel, divert</b>, ποταμὸν ἐς τὸ πεδίον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Ἰβηρική” 78, compare 12th c.AD: Tzetzes, Joannes Grammaticus “Historiarum variarum chiliades” 1.823 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]: <i>metaphorically</i>, <b>divert</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.4Mac.1.29 (conjecture), 4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 4.12; νοήματα εἰς τὸν ἐρώμενον 5th c.AD: Hermias Alexandrinus Philosophus “in Platonis Phaedrum scholia” p.165 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus \">LXX+4th c.BC+</a>]:—<i>middle</i>, φορὰν εἰς βαναύσους τέχνας μετοχετευσάμενοι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.637 \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]:—<i>passive</i>, ἐς ἡδονὰς ἀπὸ τῶν καλῶν μ. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Herodianus Grammaticus 1.3.1. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20726":"<b> μετρ-ητέον</b>, <br /> <b>one must measure</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20727":"<b> μὴ οὐ</b> <br /> is used of an apprehended <i>negative</i>: <br /><Level2><b>__I</b></Level2> with finite forms of the Verb, after Verbs expressing <b>fear</b> or <b>apprehension</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"compare (μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 8): \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>] <br /><Level4><b>__I.a</b></Level4> mostly with <i>subjunctive</i>, δείδω μὴ οὔ τίς τοι ὑπόσχηται τόδε ἔργον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.39, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.9, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.53, 57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89d, etc.: after historical tenses, with _optative_, ἠθύμησάν τινες ἐννοούμενοι μὴ οὐκ ἔχοιεν ὁπόθεν λαμβάνοιεν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.5.3, etc.\">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]: with <i>future</i> <i>optative</i> representing <i>future</i> <i>indicative</i> in indirect speech, μὴ οὐκ ὀρθῶς αὐτὸ ποιήσοις [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 15d. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>] <br /><Level4><b>__I.b</b></Level4> with <i>indicative</i>, ὁρᾶτε μὴ οὐκ ἐμοὶ μάλιστα τῶν πολιτῶν προσήκει [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.103, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Laches” 196c, Alcaeus2.139d. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__I.2</b></Level3> without introductory Verb, with <i>subjunctive</i>, μή νύ τοι οὐ χραίσμῃ [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.28, compare 566, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 982 \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]; also to suggest hesitation, <b>perhaps</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"compare (μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 9), μὴ οὐ τοῦτο ᾖ τὸ χρηστήριον 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.79, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 67b, “Smp.” 194c, 214c, etc.: also with _indicative_, μὴ τοῦτο οὐ καλῶς ὡμολογήσαμεν [prev. author] “Men.” 89c. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__I.3</b></Level3> μή is sometimes doubled, irregularly, for μὴ οὐ, ἐθαύμαζε δ᾽ εἴτις.. φοβοῖτο, μὴ ὁ γενόμενος καλὸς κἀγαθὸς.. μὴ τὴν μεγίστην χάριν ἔχοι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.7, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> after a <i>negative</i> expressed or implied: <br /><Level3><b>__II.1</b></Level3> with <i>infinitive</i>, <br /><Level4><b>__II.1.a</b></Level4> after Verbs of <b>hindering, denying, avoiding, needing</b>, when these Verbs are themselves negatived or questioned, οὐκέτι ἀνεβάλλοντο μὴ οὐ τὸ πᾶν μηχανήσασθαι <b>they no longer hesitated to try every expedient</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.88, compare 8.100, 119 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; τί δῆτα μέλλεις μὴ οὐ γεγωνίσκει; [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 627, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 540, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 320, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.2, 4.3.8, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 304c, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.24; οὐκ ἀνατίθεμαι μὴ οὐ καλῶς λέγεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Meno” 89d, compare “Phd.” 87a; πολλοῦ δέω μὴ οὐ δύο γε φεύγειν [prev. author] “Euthd.” 297b \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; τίνος ἐνδέομεν μὴ οὐ πανσυδίᾳ χωρεῖ; [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 797 (_anapaest meter_)\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; after ὥστε, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.57 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]: with the <i>Article</i>, οὐκ ἐναντιώσομαι τὸ μὴ οὐ γεγωνεῖν πᾶν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 787, compare 918, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 283, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1176; αὐτὴν οὐ μισοῦντ᾽ ἐκείνην τὴν πόλιν τὸ μὴ οὐ μεγάλην εἶναι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 37 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; τοῖς θεοῖς οὐδὲν ἂν ἔχοιμεν μέμψασθαι τὸ μὴ οὐχί.. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.42, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 13a, etc.; compare μή 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 5b. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>] <br /><Level4><b>__II.1.b</b></Level4> after Verbs and phrases signifying <b>impossibility, impropriety, reluctance</b>, when not negatived, ἄνδρα δ᾽ οὐκ ἔστι μὴ οὐ κακὸν ἔμμεναι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 5.10; δεινὸν ἐδόκεε εἶναι μὴ οὐ λαβεῖν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187; ἔστι οὐδεμία μηχανὴ μὴ οὐκ ἀπολωλέναι [prev. author] 2.181, compare 3.51, 7.5; οὐδεὶς ἂν τολμήσειεν μὴ οὐ χρῆσθαι τῷ νόμῳ 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.87, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.60; αἰσχύνη ἦν μὴ οὐ συσπουδάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.11; αἰσχρόν ἐστι μὴ οὐκ ἄλλας πληγὰς ἐμβάλλειν τῷ υἱεῖ [prev. author] “Lac.” 6.2 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; οὐδεὶς οἷός τ᾽ ἐστὶν ἄλλως λέγων μὴ οὐ (<b>nemo potest non</b>) καταγέλαστος εἶναι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 509a \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]: after an implied <i>negative</i>, μὴ οὐχὶ παντὶ τρόπῳ ἐλέγχειν μαλθακοῦ εἶναι ἀνδρός [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" [prev. author] “Phd.” 85c; μόνῃ τῇ μορφῇ μὴ οὐχὶ πρόβατα εἶναι διαφερόντων 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 15 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; after ὥστε, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1068, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Atheniensium” 3.8 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; μή and μὴ οὐκ in consecutive clauses, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" [prev. author] “Ap.” 34 \">Refs</a>]: with the <i>Article</i>, οὐκ ἀνέξομαι τὸ μὴ οὐ.. τιμᾶν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 914 \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]; οὐδείς γέ μ᾽ ἂν πείσειεν.. τὸ μὴ οὐ.. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 68, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.36. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__II.2</b></Level3> less frequently with <i>participle</i> after a <i>negative</i> expressed or implied, οὔκων δίκαιον [ἀνδριάντα] ἱστάναι.. μὴ οὐκ ὑπερβαλλόμενον τοῖσι ἔργοισι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.110, compare 6.9, 106; δυσάλγητος γὰρ ἂν εἴην τοιάνδε μὴ οὐ κατοικτείρων ἕδραν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 13, compare 221, “OC” 360, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 10.47 (uncertain reading), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Lysis” 212d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 213.5: hence \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__II.3</b></Level3> = {εἰ μή}, <b>except</b>, πόλεις.. χαλεπαὶ λαβεῖν, μὴ οὐ χρόνῳ καὶ πολιορκίᾳ [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.123. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__III</b></Level2> in questions expecting an affirmative answer, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Judg.14.3, NT.1Cor.9.4, 5th-4th c.BC: Aesopus Fabularum Scriptor 404. \">LXX+NT+5th c.BC+</a>]","G20728":"<b> μήλωσις</b>, εως, ἡ, <br /> <b>probing</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀγμῶν” 31 (_plural_), 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.17 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; <b>use of the probe</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐν κεφαλη—ͅ τρωμάτων” 10. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20729":"<b> μηνίᾱμα</b>, ατος, τό, <br /> ={μήνιμα}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.40.5. \">LXX</a>]","G20730":"<b> μηνιάω</b>, <br /> ={μηνίω}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.10.6, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis “Ars Rhetorica” 9.16, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.31, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 6.17 \">LXX+1st c.BC+</a>]: <i>Epic dialect</i> <i>3rd.pers.</i> <i>plural</i> <i>present</i> μηνιόωσιν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 2.247; πρός τι 2nd c.AD(?): Charito Scriptor Eroticus 1.2. \">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]","G20731":"<b> μηνῑτής</b>, οῦ, ὁ, <br /> <b>wrathful man</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 4.5.18 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>] (-υτής Schweigh.).","G20732":"<b> μὴπολλάκις</b>, <br /> <b>lest perchance</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.13, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 361c, “R.” 424c, +others \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20733":"<b> μηρο-ρᾰφής</b>, ές, <br /> <i>falsa lectio</i> for{μηροτραφής} in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis ad 2nd c.AD: Dionysius Periegeta 1153. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20734":"<b> μηρυκ-ίζω</b>, <br /> = {μηρυκάομαι}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).358\">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>].","G20735":"<b> μηχᾰν-άριος</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>engineer</b>, especially for irrigation machines, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “BGU” 325.7 (3rd c.AD)\">Refs 3rd c.AD+</a>]:—written <b>μηχᾰν-άρις</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Sammelb.” 5124.147 (2nd c.AD)\">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; <b>μηκαν&lt;άρ&gt;ιος</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"[prev. work] 6915. \">Refs</a>]","G20736":"<b> μῐᾰρο-φᾰγέω</b>, <br /> <b>eat abominable meats</b>, and <b>μῐᾰρο-φᾰγία</b>, ἡ, <b>eating of abominable meats</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.4Mac.5.27. \">LXX</a>]","G20737":"<b> Μιμνέρμειον</b>, τό, <br /> <b>gymnasium named after Mimnermus,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" BMus.Inscription 1030 (from Smyrna). \">Refs</a>]","G20738":"<b> μῑσ-άρετος</b> [ᾰ], ον, <br /> <b>hating virtue</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.410, +others, LXX.4Mac.11.4. \">LXX+1st c.AD+</a>]","G20739":"<b> μίσευμα</b> [ῐ], ατος, τό, <br /> ={ἡμίσευμα}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Supp.epigram” 2.705.29 (Perga, perhaps 1st c.AD). \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20740":"<b> μισθαρχίδης</b>, ου, ὁ, <br /> <b>Son of a Placeman</b>, Comedy texts patronym. in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 597 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; compare σπουδαρχίδης.","G20741":"<b> μῑσο-ξενία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>hatred of strangers</b> or <b>guests</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Wis.19.13. \">LXX</a>]","G20742":"<b> μῑσο-πονηρέω</b>, <br /> <b>hate the wicked</b> or <b>wickedness</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 30.35, “UPZ” 2.25 (2nd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 9.39.6, LXX.2Mac.4.49, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.2; μ. τισὶν ὑπὲρ ὧν ἠδίκησαν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.10.2, compare “Vit.” 27. \">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>]","G20743":"<b> μῑσο-πονηρία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>hatred of evil</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 7, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Virtutibus et Vitiis” 1250b24, LXX.2Mac.3.1, “BGU” 1208.8 (1st c.AD), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.136, Diotogenes Philosophus cited in 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes 4.7.62, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 5, etc.; ὑπὸ τῶν Ἑλλήνων -πονηρίας ἀξιοῦσθαι 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 16.23. \">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__2</b></Level3> <b>hatred because of wickedness</b>, πρός τινα, κατά τινος, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.3.1, 17.5.5; ἐπὶ τοῖς πεπραγμένοις [prev. author] “Vit.” 60. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20744":"<b> μῑσο-πόνηρος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>hating knaves and knavery</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.218, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 1.92, LXX.Est.8.13, 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.74 W., “SIG” 780.31 (Epist. Augusti), 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.267, +others; τὸ μ. 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 32.6.6 \">LXX+4th c.BC+</a>]; epithet of God, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Tab.Defix.Aud.” 271.36 (Hadrumetum, 3rd c.AD)\">Refs 3rd c.AD+</a>]. <i>adverb</i> -ρως [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “PMagd.” 14.8 (3rd c.BC), 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 30.32.5, Pseudo-Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Fluv.” 11.3, etc.\">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]; simply, <b>with hostile sentiments</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.313f. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20745":"<b> μίσυβρῐς</b> [μῑ], ιος, ὁ, ἡ, <br /> <b>hating insolence</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.3Mac.6.9. \">LXX</a>]","G20746":"<b> Μίτρα</b>, ης, ἡ, <br /> the Persian Aphrodite, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.131 (by confusion with Μίθρας)\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>].","G20747":"<b> μνημον-ευτός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>that can be remembered</b>: τὰ μ. <b>objects of memory</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1367a24, 1370b1, “Mem.” 449b9, 450a24. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]","G20748":"<b> μνησῐ-θεος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>remembering God</b>, frequently as <i>proper name</i>,[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Cratylus” 394e. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> = {ἄρκευθος}, Ps.-[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.75. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__II.2</b></Level3> = {βούφθαλμον}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" [prev. author] 3.139. \">Refs</a>]","G20749":"<b> μνησῐκᾰκ-ία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>remembrance of wrongs</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.78, 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 16.9.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.860a. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20750":"<b> μοιχ-αίνω</b>, <br /> ={μοιχεύω}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 118.5. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20751":"<b> μοιχ-εύτρια</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>adulteress</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 191e, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.60f. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20752":"<b> μόλῠβ-ος</b>, ὁ, <br /> ={μόλιβος}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Eze.27.12, “Periplus Maris Rubri [1st c.AD]” 49. \">LXX</a>]","G20753":"<b> μολῡνοπραγμονέομαι</b>, <br /> <i>passive</i>, <b>get into dirty quarrels</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 382. (Comedy texts word, imitated from πολυπραγμονέω.) \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20754":"<b> μολυν-τός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>apt to make dirty</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 6th c.AD: Olympiodorus Philosophus “in Aristotelis Meteora commentaria - pars” 320.15. \">Refs 6th c.AD+</a>]","G20755":"<b> μόλ-υσμα</b>, ατος, τό, <br /> <b>spot, taint</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD: Hierocles Platonicus Philosophus “in Carmen Aureum - FPG” 26p.478M., Scholia recentia 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 576. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]","G20756":"<b> μον-ήμερος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>staying one day</b>, καταλύτης [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Wis.5.14. \">LXX</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__2</b></Level3> <b>living one day</b>, ζῷον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 5.43. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__3</b></Level3> <b>lasting one day</b>, κόλασις [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Supp.epigram” 4.648.22 (Lydia, 2nd c.AD). \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20757":"<b> μονικῶς</b>, <br /> <i>adverb</i>, uncertain meaning in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ τοῦ καθ᾽ Ὅμηρον ἀγαθοῦ βασιλέως” p.14 \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>] O.","G20758":"<b> μονῐμότης</b>, ητος, ἡ, <br /> <b>constancy, steadfastness</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Alcibiadem” p.60 C. \">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>]","G20759":"<b> μονό-ζυξ</b>, ῠγος, <br /> <b>yoked alone</b>: hence, <b>single, solitary</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 139 (Lyric poetry). \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> ξύλα μ., opposed to τετράγωνα, ={μονόξυλα}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “IG” 12(9).907 (Chalcis, 4th c.AD). \">Refs 4th c.AD+</a>]","G20760":"<b> μονο-κένταυρος</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>man with ox's head,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"“Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum”\">Refs</a>]","G20761":"<b> μον-ορύχης</b> [ῠ], ου, ὁ, <br /> <b>digging with one point</b>, ὄρυξ [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Anthologia Graeca” 6.297 (Phanias Epigrammaticus). \">Refs</a>]","G20762":"<b> μονο-φᾰγία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>eating alone</b>, a form of gluttony, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.4Mac.1.27. \">LXX</a>]","G20763":"<b> μονο-φάγος</b> [ᾰ], ον, <br /> (φαγεῖν) <b>eating alone</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Amipsias Comicus 24, LXX.4Mac.2.7, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.301d\">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>]: irregular <i>superlative</i> μονοφαγίστατος [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 923. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20764":"<b> μορφ-ήεις</b>, εσσα, εν, <br /> <b>formed</b>, λίθου of stone, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “IG” 3.716\">Refs</a>]; especially <b>well-formed, shapely</b>, ἰδεῖν μορφάεις [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 7(6).22; conjecture in 5th c.BC: Melanippides Lyricus 1. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20765":"<b> μόσχ-ευμα</b>, ατος, τό, <br /> <b>sucker taken off and planted, offset</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.2.5, “CP” 3.11.5, “PCair.Zen.” 33.4, +others (3rd c.BC), LXX.Wis.4.3, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.398, “PLond.unpublished” 2316 “A.” \">LXX+4th c.BC+</a>]","G20766":"<b> μοχθ-ίζω</b>, <br /> ={μοχθέω}, περὶ χρήμασι μ. <b>toil</b> for money, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 123.6 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; ἕλκεϊ μοχθίζοντα.. ὕδρου <b>suffering</b> from the wound.., [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.723; δαίμονι δειλῷ μ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 164; φθειρσὶ μ. 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 137; ἐτώσια μ. 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.38, 7.48; μόχθους μ. 2nd c.BC: Moschus Bucolicus 4.44: _absolutely_, 5th-6th c.AD: Orphica “Argonautica” 1071. \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]","G20767":"<b> μυελοποιός</b>, όν, <br /> <b>making marrow</b>, i. e. <b>strengthening</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" Scholia D 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.290. \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]","G20768":"<b> μῡθόλογ-ος</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>teller of legends, romancer</b>, joined with ποιητής, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" [prev. author = 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus] “R.” 392d, compare 398b, “Lg.” 664d, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.13.2, LXX.Bar.3.23; used of 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus by 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 756b6. \">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <i>adjective</i> <b>mythological</b>, μνήμη [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Aetia” 3.1.55. \">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level3><b>__II.2</b></Level3> <b>prating</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.445. \">Refs 4th c.AD+</a>]","G20769":"<b> μυκτηρι-άζω</b>, <br /> <b>μυκτηρι-ασμός</b>, <b>μυκτηρι-αστής</b>, ={μυκτηρίζω}, -ισμός, -ιστής, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Corpus Glossariorum Latinorum” \">Refs</a>]","G20770":"<b> μῠλ-ήφᾰτος</b>, ον, <br /> (θείνω) <b>bruised in a mill</b>, εἴκοσι.. μέτρα μυληφάτου ἀλφίτου ἀκτῆς [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.355, compare 3rd c.BC: Apollonius Rhodius Epicus 1.1073, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 578. \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]","G20771":"<b> μῠλόομαι</b>, <br /> <i>passive</i>, (μύλη) <b>to be hardened, cicatrized</b>, of wounds, <i>falsa lectio</i> for{ἐμυκώθη} in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.40, as read by 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>] (ἐμυλώθη· ἐτυλώθη) μυλοῦνται is probably conjecture for μολοῦνται in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Κωακαὶ προγνώσιες” 501. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20772":"<b> μῠρεψ-ία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>preparation of unguents</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Insomniis” 460a27. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]","G20773":"<b> μῠρέψ-ιον</b>, τό, <br /> <b>prepared unguent</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Isa.57.9. \">LXX+2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20774":"<b> μυρίνινος</b>, <br /> <i>falsa lectio</i> for{σμύρνινος}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Est.2.12. \">LXX</a>]","G20775":"<b> μῡρῐο-πάλαι</b> [ᾰ], <br /> <i>adverb</i> <b>time out of mind</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 725.40 \">Refs</a>]; compare τρισμυριοπάλαι. <br /><b> τρισμῡριό-πᾰλαι</b>, <br />&nbsp;&nbsp;<i>adverb</i> <b>thirty-thousand-times long-ago,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1156 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; cf. τρίπαλαι, τετράπαλαι.","G20776":"<b> μῡρῐό-της</b>, ητος, ἡ, <br /> ={μυριάς}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Wis.12.22. \">LXX</a>]","G20777":"<b> μῠρ-ισμός</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>an anointing</b>, Antig.Car. cited in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 12.547f, LXX.Jdt.16.8. \">LXX+2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20778":"<b> μυρμηκ-ίᾱσις</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>irritation of the skin</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 3rd c.AD: Philumenus Medicus “de Venenatis Animalibus” 14.2 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; also, ={νάρκη}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus s. Hymns of  see \">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>]","G20779":"<b> μυρμηκο-ειδής</b>, ές, <br /> <b>like an ant</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus\">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>] see entry {σίφων; μ. ὁρᾶσθαι} [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"Cass  “Pr.” 19. \">Refs</a>]","G20780":"<b> μῠρο-βρᾰχής</b> <br /> or <b>μῠρο-βρεχής</b>, ές, (βρέχω) <b>wet with unguent</b>, κόμη [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.3Mac.4.6, compare 2nd c.AD: Suetonius Grammaticus et Historicus Latinus “Aug.” 86 \">LXX+2nd c.AD+</a>]:—also <b>μῠρό-βροχος</b>, ον, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 3.16. \">Refs 3rd c.AD+</a>]","G20781":"<b> μῠσᾰρ-ία</b>, ἡ, <br /> <b>loathsomeness</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd-3rd c.AD: Symmachus LXX.Eze.16.58. \">LXX+2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20782":"<b> μῠσάρχης</b>, ου, ὁ, <br /> (μύσος) <b>the originator of a foul deed</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.2Mac.5.24. \">LXX</a>]","G20783":"<b> μῠσερός</b>, ά, όν, <br /> late form of μυσαρός, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 4.269, “Etymologicum Magnum” 535.32, variant in LXX.Lev.18.23 \">LXX+4th c.AD+</a>].","G20784":"<b> μυστάδης·</b> εἶδός τι, καὶ φατρία μάντεων, <br /> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus \">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>]","G20785":"<b> μυστηρι-κός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>of</b> or <b>for mysteries</b>, χοιρία [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 747. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20786":"<b> μυστικός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>connected with the mysteries</b>, τέλος [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 387 \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]; μ. Ἴακχος the <b>mystic</b> chant Iacchus, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.65; χοῖροι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 764; αὔρα τις εἰσέπνευσε -ωτάτη [prev. author] “Ra.” 314; βίοτος μ. “IG” 3.172.6; μ. λόγοι 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.46 W.\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; τὰ μ. <b>the mysteries</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.28, 60 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; ἡ -κή (i.e. παράδοσις) <b>mystical doctrine</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.779 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, compare [same work] “in Ti.” 3.12 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator; τὸ θεῖον καὶ μ. 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 213 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; οἱ μ., ={μύσται}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.1.29 \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]: <i>comparative</i> -ώτερος [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Salt.” 59 \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]: <i>superlative</i> -ώτατος [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ra.” [same place], 5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 111 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]. <i>adverb</i> - κῶς, δικάζειν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 8.123 (perhaps μυστικῶν\">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>] <b>in cases relating to the mysteries); mystically</b>, μ. καὶ τελεστικῶς [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Hermogenes Rhetor “περὶ ἰδεῶν” 1.6, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 5.77, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 4. \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <b>private, secret</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 4.2.7 (_comparative_)\">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]. <i>adverb</i> - κῶς [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.3.9, LXX.3Mac.3.10, 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 46.11 \">LXX+1st c.BC+</a>]: <i>comparative</i> -ώτερον, <b>scribere</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC: Cicero, M. Tullius Orator et Philosophus “Epistulae ad Atticum” 6.4.3. \">Refs 1st c.BC+</a>]","G20787":"<b> μύστῐς</b>, ῐδος, <br /> <i>feminine</i> of μύστης, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “IG” 3.914, 3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “de Antro Nympharum - Porphyrii Opuscula” 18 \">Refs 3rd c.AD+</a>]: usually <i>metaphorically</i>, <b>initiate</b> or <b>initiator</b>, μ. νάματος ἡ Κύπρις [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Anacreontea” 4.12; πενίης μύστι, λάγυνε “Anthologia Graeca” 9.229 (6th c.AD: Marcus Argentarius Epigrammaticus)\">Refs 6th c.AD+</a>]; μ. τῆς τοῦ θεοῦ ἐπιστήμης, of Σοφία, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Wis.8.4; [ψυχὴ] τῶν τελείων μ. τελετῶν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.173. \">LXX+1st c.AD+</a>]","G20788":"<b> μώϊον</b>, τό, <br /> Egyptian word for a <b>receptacle, box</b> or <b>jar,</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" PHib. 1.49.8, “PPetr.” 3p.186 (3rd c.BC)\">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]; χαλκωμάτων [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “PSI” 4.428.78 (3rd c.BC)\">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]; probably also a measure of capacity, ἀχύρου μώϊα μα [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “Ostr.Bodl.” i231 (2nd c.BC)\">Refs 2nd c.BC+</a>]: <i>plural</i> written μώεια [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “PCair.Zen.” 167.7 (3rd c.BC)\">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]; <i><i>singular</i></i> written μούειον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “POxy.” 146.3 (6th c.AD)\">Refs 6th c.AD+</a>]; compare μώστιον.","G20789":"<b> μώκ-ημα</b>, ατος, τό, <br /> <b>mockery</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.31(34).18 \">LXX</a>] (<i>plural</i>, variant μωμ-).","G20790":"<b> μωκός</b>, ὁ, <br /> <b>mocker</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 491b17, “Etymologicum Magnum” 593.7 \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]: as <i>adjective</i>, φίλος μ. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.36(33).6. \">LXX</a>]","G20791":"<b> μωλωπ-ικός</b>, ή, όν, <br /> <b>suitable for weals</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 19.139. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]","G20792":"<b> μώμ-ημα</b>, ατος, τό, <br /> <b>blame</b>, variant for{μώκημα}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Sir.31(34).18. \">LXX</a>]","G20793":"<b> μῶν</b>, <br /> <i>adverb</i>, <i>contraction</i> for μὴ οὖν, μῶν χαραδριὸν περνᾷς· [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 6th c.BC: Hipponax Iambographus 52: frequently in Trag., Comedy texts, and 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus \">Refs 6th c.BC+</a>]; usually in questions to which a <i>negative</i> answer is suggested, <b>surely not</b>? μῶν ἄλγος ἴσχει; you are <b>not</b> in pain, <b>are you</b>? [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 734, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 676, 754, “Hel.” 1198, 5th c.BC: Achaeus Tragicus 9, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 69, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 310d.\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>] —Its origin from μὴ οὖν was forgotten, hence μῶν οὖν..; in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 177, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 82 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; μῶν οὖν δῆτα..; [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 845: sometimes also μῶν μὴ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84c, “R.” 505c \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; also μῶν οὐ..; suggesting an affirmative answer, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 417, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1729 (Lyric poetry), 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 234a, etc. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]","G20794":"<b> μωρ-εύω</b>, <br /> ={μωραίνω}, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Isa.44.25. \">LXX</a>]","G20795":"<b> μωρο-πόνηρος</b>, ον, <br /> <b>stupidly wicked</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus 2.58, Polemo Physiognomonicus 68. \">Refs 4th c.AD+</a>]","G20796":"<b> ναῦλος</b>, ὁ, <br /> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus (see. below), “IG” 22.1672.126,159, “SIG” 1262.6 (Smyrna, 1st c.AD), “Comica Adespota” 286\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; also ἡ, <i>accusative</i> to [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 272 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; and <b>ναῦλον</b>, τό (see. below), compare ναῦλλον:— <b>passage-money, fare</b> or <b>freight</b>, ἔκβαιν᾽, ἀπόδος τὸν ν., says [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Charon Historicus, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 270; τῆς πόλεως ναῦλον τελούσης τοῖς ἄγουσι τοὺς λίθους “IPE” 12.32 B 50 (Olbia, 3rd c.BC)\">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; ναῦλον συνθέσθαι to agree upon one's <b>fare</b>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.1.12; τὸν. τῶν ξύλων παρασχεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 49.26, compare “IG” [same places]; τὸ ν. ἀποστερεῖν 4th-3rd c.BC: Dinarchus Orator 1.56 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; παραπόλλυμι τὸ ν. Arisipp. cited in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.439e; ἔδωκε τὸ ν. αὐτοῦ LXX.Jonah.1.3; λαλῶν τὰ ν. 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.21; ἔδωκα αὐτῷ τὰ ν. “Sammelb.” 3553; τὸ δὲ ν. διωρθωσάμεθα ὑπὲρ αὐτῶν “PCair.Zen.” 52.13 (3rd c.BC)\">LXX+4th c.BC+</a>]; συνέβη ναῦλον ἡμῖν προσάγεσθαι τοῦ.. πλοίου we were charged for the <b>hire</b> of the boat, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"[prev. work] 368.27 (3rd c.BC). \">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level4><b>__b</b></Level4> ν. πλοίου name of a tax paid for the use of state-provided boats, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “BGU” 645.16 (2nd c.AD), “PSI” 8.960.15 (4th c.AD)\">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]; ἀποδιαγράψειν τὸ συναχθησόμενον ν. ἐπὶ τὴν βασιλικὴν τράπεζαν [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" “PTheb.Bank” 12.7 (2nd c.BC),compare “Sammelb.” 6954. \">Refs 2nd c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <b>freight, cargo</b> of ships, τὸ ν. σφετερίζεσθαι [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 32.2. \">Refs 4th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__III</b></Level2> <b>rent</b> of a tenement, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 1.75. \">Refs 2nd c.AD+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__IV</b></Level2> ναῦλα, τά, = ἐφόδια, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus (ascribed to Aeschylus by Cyrilli Glossarium in codex Laur.57.39). \">Refs 5th c.AD+</a>]","G20797":"<b> νάφθᾰ</b>, ἡ <br /> (τό, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 700.56)\">Refs</a>], <b>naphtha</b> (Persian <b>naft</b>), [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.73, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.3a \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]:—also <b>νάφθας</b>, ὁ, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 16.1.15; _accusative_ νάφθαν LXX.Dan.3.64 \">LXX+1st c.BC+</a>]; <i>genitive</i> νάφθα Str.[same place], [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Alexander” 35. \">Refs 1st c.AD+</a>]","G20798":"<b> νεικ-έω</b>, <br /> <i>future</i> <b>-έσω</b>[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.115: \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]<i>aorist</i> ἐνείκεσα, <i>Epic dialect</i> νείκεσσα or νείκεσα, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 3.38, 10.158 \">Refs</a>]: also <i>present</i> <b>νεικείω</b> [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2.277 \">Refs</a>], etc.; <i>subjunctive</i> νεικείῃσι, νεικείῃ,[<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 1.579, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 332 \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]; <i>participle</i> νεικείουσα [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.35 \">Refs 3rd c.BC+</a>]: <i>imperfect</i> νείκειον [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.26; iterative νεικείεσκον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.241 \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]: (νεῖκος):—<b>quarrel, wrangle with</b> one, μή μοι ὀπίσσω νεικείῃ [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.189 \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]; ἔριδος πέρι θυμοβόροιο νεικεῦσ᾽ ἀλλήλῃσι <b>quarrel</b> one <b>with</b> another, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.254; ἐνείκεον εἵνεκα ποινῆς 18.498: with _accusative_ _cognate_, νείκεα.. νεικεῖν ἀλλήλοισιν ἐναντίον 20.252 \">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]; νεικέσκομεν οἴω, we two alone <b>strove with him,</b> is probably corrupt in [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.512 (νικάσκομεν 3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus)\">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]: <i>absolutely</i> in <i>participle</i> νεικέων [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.55. \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]<br /><Level2><b>__II</b></Level2> <i>transitive</i>, <b>chide, rail at, upbraid,</b> with <i>accusative</i> <i>person</i>, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.521, +others; Ἀγαμέμνονα ν. μύθῳ 2.224; αἰσχροῖς, ὀνειδείοις, χολωτοῖσιν ἐπέεσσιν, 3.38, 21.480, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.225, etc.\">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]; ὃς νείκεσσε θεάς.., τὴν δ᾽ ᾔνησ᾽ [Paris] <b>insulted</b> the goddesses (Hera and Athena), but praised the other [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\"(from Aphrodite), 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.29.\">Refs 8th c.BC+</a>]—<i>Epic dialect</i> Verb, used twice by [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus, 8.125, 9.55 \">Refs 5th c.BC+</a>]; not in <i>Attic dialect</i>: in later Prose, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.Pro.10.12, +others \">LXX</a>]","G20799":"<b> νεκρ-ῐμαῖος</b>, α, ον, <br /> ={θνησιμαῖος}, of animals, [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" 2nd c.AD: Aquila LXX.Deu.14.8, Scholia 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 538, “OGI” 629.158 (Palmyra, 2nd c.AD), 1st c.AD: Erotianus Grammaticus and 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus\">LXX+5th c.BC+</a>] see entry {κενέβρεια}; also of a human being, τὸ ν. [<a href=\"javascript:void(0)\" title=\" LXX.1Ki.13.25. \">LXX</a>]"}